<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- NuGet packaging metadata -->
    <Description>OIDC/JWT implementation for SharedKernel security: OidcUserContext, OidcTenantProvider, SecurityOptions (AddValidatedOptions) with configurable ClaimMapping, AddSharedKernelSecurity and AddAzureB2CAuthentication DI extensions, opt-in DPoP (RFC 9449) sender-constrained tokens and RFC 7662-shaped token revocation via SecurityAuthenticationBuilder, structured security-audit logging. Depends on SharedKernel.Security.Abstractions and Microsoft.Identity.Web.</Description>
    <PackageTags>shared-kernel;security;oidc;jwt;azure-b2c;identity;dependency-injection;dpop</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>4.0.0 (BREAKING default-behavior change, fintech/FAPI-hardening pass): SecurityOptions.Jwt.ValidAlgorithms now defaults to ["PS256", "ES256"] and IS enforced (previously defined but not wired) via both AddSharedKernelSecurity and AddAzureB2CAuthentication — a token signed with any other algorithm (including "none") is now rejected. Many real-world identity providers (Entra ID, Auth0, Okta) sign access tokens with RS256 by default; if yours does, you MUST explicitly widen Jwt.ValidAlgorithms to include "RS256" (or your provider's actual algorithm) after upgrading, or every previously-valid token will be rejected. A rejection is audit-logged via the new JwtSigningAlgorithmRejected (EventId 12104) event. DpopOptions.ValidAlgorithms is enforced identically inside DpopProofValidator, checked before any other DPoP binding. DpopProofValidator now also validates the RFC 9449 §4.3 "ath" (access-token-hash) binding — a DPoP proof lacking a correct "ath" claim is now rejected (previously never checked); this only affects hosts that opted into .RequireDpop&lt;TReplayCache&gt;(). Adds the opt-in revocation-check caching seam: IRevocationCheckCache, RevocationCheckCacheOptions, CachingTokenRevocationCheck, and SecurityAuthenticationBuilder.WithRevocationCheckCaching&lt;TCache&gt;() (must be chained after .WithRevocationCheck&lt;TCheck&gt;()) — fully opt-in, no behavior change for a consumer that does not call it. See WO-060 (P-385/P-387/P-388). 3.0.0 (breaking, tracks SharedKernel.Security.Abstractions 3.0.0): OidcUserContext now resolves the six new step-up-authentication IUserContext members (AuthenticationMethods/AuthContextClassReference/AuthTime from amr/acr/auth_time claims, IsSenderConstrained from a successful DPoP proof validation). Adds SecurityAuthenticationBuilder (returned in place of a bare IServiceCollection, source-compatible) with opt-in .RequireDpop&lt;TReplayCache&gt;() and .WithRevocationCheck&lt;TCheck&gt;() extension points. See WO-058 (P-375/P-376/P-379). 2.0.0 (breaking, tracks SharedKernel.Security.Abstractions 2.0.0): OidcUserContext now resolves IdentityKind (Anonymous/User/ServicePrincipal) and Permissions/HasPermission from the scope/scp claim. Claim-type resolution (Email/Name/Role/Permission) is now configurable per identity provider via SecurityOptions.ClaimMapping, matching modern short-name OIDC defaults instead of legacy ClaimTypes.* URIs. See WO-057 (P-366/P-367/P-368/P-371).</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SharedKernel.Security.Abstractions\SharedKernel.Security.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\01.Core\SharedKernel.Configuration\SharedKernel.Configuration.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer: JWT Bearer validation.
      AOT note: JWT token parsing uses internal reflection in some code paths.
      Encapsulated behind IUserContext so the AOT blast radius is limited to the DI registration path.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" />
    <!--
      Microsoft.Identity.Web: Azure B2C / Entra External ID authority/audience wiring.
      AOT note: Microsoft.Identity.Web is not fully AOT-safe. Isolated to AddAzureB2CAuthentication
      extension only. A swap to a lighter JWT-only path does not require abstraction changes.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Identity.Web" />
    <!--
      DEVOPS SECURITY OVERRIDE (DV-005): Microsoft.Identity.Web 3.10.0 transitively pulls
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection 9.0.0, which pulls System.Security.Cryptography.Xml
      9.0.0 — flagged with 8 known high-severity .NET DoS/security-feature-bypass advisories
      (GHSA-23rf-6693-g89p, -37gx-xxp4-5rgx, -6588-8gv4-xfgh, -8q5v-6pqq-x66h, -cvvh-rhrc-wg4q,
      -g8r8-53c2-pm3f, -mmjf-rqrv-855v, -w3x6-4m5h-cxqf; all fixed at 10.0.10+/10.0.6+ depending
      on advisory). CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled is deliberately OFF repo-wide (see
      Directory.Packages.props), so the fix is this explicit, reviewable top-level PackageReference
      pinned to the patched 10.0.11 in Directory.Packages.props — never a blanket transitive pin.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Grants SharedKernel.Security.Oidc.Tests direct access to internal types (DpopProofValidator,
      RevocationCheckRunner, DpopClaimTypes) so the DPoP (RFC 9449) proof-validation algorithm and the
      revocation-check fail-closed contract can be proven directly (WO-058, T-22/T-23/T-27/T-28) —
      mirrors SharedKernel.Security.ApiKey's identical InternalsVisibleTo grant for ConstantTimeKeyComparer.
    -->
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleTo">
      <_Parameter1>SharedKernel.Security.Oidc.Tests</_Parameter1>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
